# Gauntlet G2+G3 fresh-eyes pass — debrief, round 4 (after the round-3 fix batch)

Scope: re-read every file this round touched, top to bottom —
`src/debrief/adapters/ggufbench_store.py`, `src/debrief/use_cases.py`,
`tests/test_bench.py`, `tests/test_propose.py` — plus the boundary callers
(`src/debrief/mcp_server.py::_resolve_within`, `adapters/buildwright_log.py`),
independent of the batch memory that wrote them.

## Fixed findings re-verified through the public MCP path

- [round-3 G2-1, nested symlink indirection] `bench.query {"path": "r1"}` with
  `<root>/r1/results.json` → outside file (winning LEAKED-model row):
  `source_unparseable`, findings `[]`, message "a run's results.json escapes the
  ggufbench artifact root", no `LEAKED-model` anywhere in the response
  (`harness/logs/debrief/demo.log`, "gauntlet round-3 findings" section).
  `routing.propose` over the same artifact refuses with the same code; the leak
  cannot reach the proposal ranking. Both access shapes (artifact-root scan and
  the run dir requested directly) are regression-locked:
  `tests/test_bench.py::test_symlinked_results_file_outside_the_root_refuses_the_scan[artifact_root_scan|run_dir_requested]`,
  `test_bench_query_tool_refuses_a_symlink_escaping_below_the_requested_path`,
  `tests/test_propose.py::test_symlink_escaping_the_bench_root_never_reaches_the_proposal[*]`.
  Mechanism checked by reading the code, not just the probe: `_results_files`
  resolves the store root once, and every candidate (`root / RESULTS_FILE` and
  each `iterdir` entry) goes through `_contained_results`, which resolves and
  requires `is_relative_to(root)` before `_read_run` is ever handed a path.
- [round-3 G2-2, prefixed role token] step line `role=BW_ROLE_WORKER` →
  `+ BW_ROLE_WORKER=llama-3.1-8b-instruct-q4_k_m`, no `BW_ROLE_BW_ROLE_`
  anywhere in the answer (`demo.log`, last probe). Regression table covers the
  canonical prefix, its lowercase form, and both bare spellings
  (`tests/test_propose.py::test_role_tokens_carrying_the_env_prefix_still_render_a_pasteable_diff[*]`);
  dedup now happens on the canonical name, so `worker` and `BW_ROLE_WORKER`
  failures in one log collapse to a single proposal (improvement, not just a
  cosmetic fix).

## New-angle scan (tree-structure vectors beyond the reported probe)

Residual probes run against the fixed store (all PASS, transient self-deleting
probe, output in the session archive):

1. symlink loop (`results.json` → itself) → `source_unparseable`, no crash
   (resolve() breaks the loop, stat() raises ELOOP, findings() maps OSError).
2. run *directory* symlink escaping the root (`<root>/r1` → outside dir with a
   results file) → `source_unparseable` (the fix covers any nesting depth, not
   only the one-level-down case the round-3 probe used).
3. `results.json` symlink → directory inside the root → skipped as "no results
   here" (`bench_source_missing` when nothing else exists), no crash, no
   directory read.
4. two-hop symlink chain fully inside the root (`results.json` → link → real)
   → still read: the boundary refuses escapes, not symlinks
   (also pinned by `tests/test_bench.py::test_symlinked_results_file_inside_the_root_is_still_read`).
5. degenerate role token `BW_ROLE_` → renders text only
   (`- BW_ROLE_=m-out` / `+ BW_ROLE_=m-keeper`), never double-prefixed, and the
   answer still carries the "not applied" note — R2 holds for every token shape.

## Defended findings (recorded per protocol, no action)

1. TOCTOU window between `resolve()`/`is_relative_to()` and `read_text()` — a
   local attacker able to swap files *under the evidence root between* the two
   calls could redirect one read outside. Defended: identical window to the
   already-accepted `_resolve_within` design (resolve-then-read at the adapter
   boundary, rounds 1–3); the declared threat model is hostile artifact bytes /
   structure present at query time, not a concurrent local filesystem writer —
   a single-user, localhost, read-only evidence tool with no agent write path
   (R2 tripwire). Closing it needs fd-based `openat2(RESOLVE_BENEATH)` /
   `O_NOFOLLOW` plumbing, which is out of scope for the ordered one-spot fix
   and adds platform-specific code to a stdio tool. Trade-off named: strict
   path-boundary check at query time vs race-proof fds — chosen the former,
   consistent with the codebase's stated A03 convention.
2. Degenerate role token `BW_ROLE_` normalizes to an empty role and renders
   `BW_ROLE_` as the env name. Defended: text-only output (R5/R6 intact — diff
   lines still start `BW_ROLE_`), nothing applied, and the input itself
   (`role=BW_ROLE_`) is malformed; widening the adapter to reject it would add
   a validation rule for a shape no buildwright log can produce meaningfully.
3. The `except OSError` branch inside `_contained_results` maps a resolve-time
   OSError to the escape wording — slightly off-message for a non-escape I/O
   error. Defended as effectively unreachable: CPython's `Path.resolve(strict=False)`
   swallows per-component OSErrors, so unreadable components surface at the
   `stat()` in `_is_regular_file` instead, which maps to the scan-failure
   message ("could not be scanned"), matching rounds 1–2 behavior. The escape
   message only fires on the real escape path (`is_relative_to` false).

## Prior-round fixes re-checked (rounds 1–2)

NUL/absolute/`..`/empty path args → `path_outside_root`; NaN/Infinity/
-Infinity/`1e999`/huge-int scores → `source_unparseable` at the score boundary
(no fabricated `evil-model` winner); outcome tokens outside {pass, fail} →
`source_unparseable` in both adapters (no false "every logged step passed");
nesting bombs and undecodable bytes → `source_unparseable`; unreadable dirs →
structured error naming the file, never a silent skip. All re-verified over
MCP stdio in `harness/logs/debrief/demo.log` (rounds 1–2 section) — unchanged
by this round's diff. No write path exists in `src/debrief` (R2 tripwire test
green; grep over the package clean).

## G3 maintainability

- Dependency direction unchanged: domain → errors; ports → domain/errors;
  use_cases → domain/errors/ports; adapters → domain/errors; only mcp_server
  imports `mcp`. No ggufbench/buildwright knowledge leaked (ADR-0002 holds).
- The new code follows the conventions: one failure mode per named helper
  (`_contained_results`), narrow typed excepts with why-comments, `from err`
  chaining, no nested try/catch (findings() has two sequential excepts on one
  try), no mutable defaults, docstrings state why (A03, evidence-tool refusal
