# Gauntlet G2+G3 fresh-eyes pass — debrief, round 8 (after the round-7 fix batch)

Scope: the round-7 findings landed as a human fix order (G2-1 MUST-FIX: refuse
zero-step run logs at the adapter boundary; optionally cross-check the
`result:`/`failed_steps` header). Re-read top to bottom, independent of the
batch memory that wrote them:
`src/debrief/adapters/buildwright_log.py` (whole file — `_map_log`, the new
`_Step` record, `_parse_step`, `_finding`, `_optional_header`,
`_check_result_header`, `_declared_failed_steps`), the regression rows in
`tests/test_run.py` and `tests/test_propose.py`, and the boundary callers
(`src/debrief/use_cases.py::InspectRun/.ProposeRole`, `mcp_server.py`).

## Fix verification (round-7 G2-1, ordered)

- `_map_log` raises `_Unparseable` when the log has a `task:` header but zero
  `step:` lines → `source_unparseable` at the adapter boundary; a headers-only
  log can no longer answer `[]` as a structured success, and ProposeRole can no
  longer answer "no proposal: every logged step passed" for a step-stripped log
  whose own `result: fail failed_steps=01` header declares a failure.
  Regression rows: `tests/test_run.py::test_headers_only_run_log_refuses_as_unparseable`,
  `tests/test_propose.py::test_propose_does_not_claim_all_passed_for_a_step_stripped_log`.
  Re-probed over MCP stdio (`harness/logs/debrief/demo.log`, "gauntlet round-7
  findings" section): `run.inspect {"path": "headers-only.log"}` →
  `source_unparseable` ("no step: lines"); `routing.propose` over the same log
  → the structured error, the all-passed claim absent from the response.

## Fix verification (round-7 G2-2 family, optional cross-check — implemented)

- `_check_result_header` cross-checks the `result:` header against the logged
  step outcomes once every step line has parsed: `result: pass` over a logged
  failure refuses; a `failed_steps` list not matching the logged failing step
  ordinals refuses; a fail claim with no logged failing step refuses; a failure
  without a `failed_steps` list refuses; an empty list, a malformed header and
  an outcome token outside the pass/fail enum refuse. The zero-step refusal
  fires first (ordered fix semantics preserved). Regression rows:
  `tests/test_run.py::test_result_header_contradicting_the_logged_steps_refuses_the_log[*]`
  (7 reject cases) + `test_result_header_agreeing_with_the_logged_steps_still_parses` +
  `test_failed_steps_without_zero_padding_still_matches_the_logged_step`;
  `tests/test_propose.py::test_propose_does_not_claim_all_passed_for_a_log_whose_result_header_lies[*]`.
- Ordinals compare zero-padding-insensitively (`02` == `2` == `0002`), comma
  lists match multi-failure logs, and a missing `result:` header still parses
  (the header is optional; producers older than the failed_steps convention omit it).

## Residual probes (19, all PASS)

consistent fail header parses; unpadded/zero-padded ordinals match; duplicate
ordinals match; `02,04` matches a two-failure log; no result header parses;
`result: pass failed_steps=none` over an all-pass log parses; pass header over
a logged failure refuses; `failed_steps=01` naming a step that passed refuses;
`failed_steps=02,03` listing one failure too many refuses; fail without a
`failed_steps` list refuses; fail over an all-pass log refuses; empty
`failed_steps` refuses; `result: partial` (enum) refuses; space-separated
ordinal list refuses (malformed header); trailing prose after the header
refuses; headers-only log refuses before the header check; a second
`result:` header is ignored (first wins, as for every header key); a pass
header carrying an unrelated `failed_steps=99` token parses.

## G2 findings

- [G2-1 FIXED] zero-step run log → `[]` structured success / all-passed claim
  (round-7 order) — refused at the adapter boundary, regression-locked (above).
- [G2-2 FIXED, optional part implemented] `result:`/`failed_steps` header
  ignored — now cross-checked against the logged step outcomes, both refusal
  directions covered (header-passes-but-step-failed; header-failed-but-no-step)
  and regression-locked (above).
- [G2 DEFENDED: pass header carrying a `failed_steps` token parses] On a
  passing run the token is meaningless producer metadata; refusing it risks
  false refusals of legitimate logs (`failed_steps=none`), and the fabrication
  direction — a pass claim over a logged failure — refuses. Named trade-off:
  tolerance on meaningless metadata, strictness on contradictions.
- [G2 DEFENDED: duplicate `result:` headers — first one wins] Consistent with
  `_header`'s first-wins semantics for every header key (`task:`, `run:` …);
  a duplicated header is producer noise, not a contradiction an attacker can
  aim (the *checked* first header still must agree with the steps).
- [G2 DEFENDED: fail header without a `failed_steps` list refuses — a false
  refusal risk if real producers omit it] The adapter owns the format
  (module docstring declares the `result: <outcome> failed_steps=<list>`
  shape); a failure claim that cannot be cross-checked is refused rather than
  trusted, which is the same direction as the outcome-enum boundary. Trade-off
  named: strictness over leniency for an unverifiable failure claim.
- Carried defenses re-checked and unchanged: invalid DEBRIEF_LOG_LEVEL
  (fail-fast operator error), unbounded read_text (bounded by the producer,
  operator-configured local root), TOCTOU window (needs openat2, out of scope),
  TaskSignature.key render ambiguity (display-only, comparability uses the
  frozen dataclass).

## G3 maintainability

- Dependency direction unchanged and re-checked by imports: domain → errors;
  ports → domain/errors; use_cases → domain/errors/ports; adapters →
  domain/errors; only mcp_server imports `mcp`; no write path anywhere (R2
  tripwire `tests/test_bench.py::test_no_debrief_module_contains_a_write_path`
